<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Uda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/866">866</a>–931</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Uda-tennō, Sadami, Minamoto no Sadami</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Uda was the 59th emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> according to the traditional order of succession, reigning from 887 to 897. A son of Emperor Kōkō, he had been demoted to commoner status as Minamoto no Sadami in 884 and was restored as an imperial prince only in 887, just before his father's death. His attempt in the Akō controversy to make the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a>'s office merely honorific failed, and only after Mototsune died in 891 could he rule directly, promoting men outside the main Fujiwara line such as Sugawara no Michizane and ending missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>. He abdicated in 897 in favour of his son Daigo, became a monk and died in 931.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-uda.jpg" alt="Emperor Uda" width="330" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown; derivative work by bamse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Uda_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uda was born on 10 June 866, a son of Emperor Kokō, whose mother was Empress Dowager Hanshi. Kokō had taken the throne after Emperor Yozei was forced to abdicate. In June 884 he gave the surname Minamoto to 26 princes and princesses of his line, demoting them to commoners, to reduce state expenses and their political influence. When Kokō fell seriously ill in 887, the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-mototsune">Fujiwara no Mototsune</a> stated the emperor's preference for Sadami over Prince Sadayasu, Yozei's brother. Sadami was the adopted son of Mototsune's half-sister Fujiwara no Yoshiko, a powerful court lady who strongly recommended him. He was made an imperial prince and crown prince, and was enthroned on 17 November 887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Uda lacked experience, and Mototsune, regent for four emperors, held all power. Shortly after the enthronement Uda confirmed him as regent, then with Tachibana no Hiromi issued a second edict equating the kanpaku with the honorific title ako, which would have sidelined him. The court, dominated by the Fujiwara, did not accept this, and in June 888 he withdrew the edict. After Mototsune died in 891 Uda ruled directly. He founded Ninna-ji as an imperial temple in 894 and promoted men outside the main Fujiwara line, such as Minamoto no Yoshiari, Sugawara no Michizane and Fujiwara no Yasunori.</p><p>The reign saw the suspension of missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the compiling of the national histories Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku and Ruiju Kokushi, the consolidation or abolition of numerous government offices, and edicts protecting peasant land rights against powerful families and monasteries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 4 August 897 Uda proclaimed Crown Prince Atsuhito of age and abdicated the same day. It was long believed he did so to devote himself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, but the act secured the succession in his own line before the Fujiwara could press a candidate of their own. He took the tonsure at Tō-ji in 899 and entered Ninna-ji as a monk. In January 901 Michizane was exiled to Dazaifu on suspicion of trying to put Uda's son Prince Tokiyo, his son-in-law, on the throne.</p><p>After the death of Fujiwara no Tokihira in 909 Uda regained influence, and he held a poetry contest at the palace on 22 April 913. He acted as regent to Emperor Suzaku after Daigo died in 930 and died on 3 September 931. His diary, the Kanpyo Gyoki, is an important source for the period, and his descendants became the Uda Genji.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Uda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An emperor born of another mother could slip their hold: <b>Emperor Uda</b> regained much of the government, and Emperor Go-Sanjō, from 1068, curbed the Fujiwara and began the rule of retired emperors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Ninna-ji Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35,135.77&amp;year=908" aria-label="Heian-kyō on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7991 898 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Uda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35,135.77&amp;year=908">What was here in 908 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
